Q: How to find memory leaks in vue js project? Does Vue js handle those leaks on its own? A: <p><a href="https://github.com/nolanlawson/fuite/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Fuite</a> is a great tool to find memory leaks. They have a nice <a href="https://youtu.be/H0BHL2lo89M" rel="nofollow noreferrer">introductory video</a> linked in their readme as well. Basically it crawls your app for internal links, and then repeatedly visits them (for example from <code>/</code> to <code>about</code>, then the native &quot;Back&quot; navigation, then repeats <code>N</code> number of times). After each visit, it checks if any event listeners or DOM nodes were left detached. You'll get a nice readable report by the end of it.</p>
<p>You can run it from the CLI <code>npx fuite http://localhost:8080</code> or if you have authentication guards or some custom interactions you'd like to define, then you can create &quot;scenarios&quot; for Fuite to go through.</p>
<p>Here's a simple custom scenario, that extends the fuite default scenario, by adding logging in. It then dumps the results into a JSON file. Make sure you've got the <code>logs</code> directory created beforehand. You'd also need to install <code>fuite</code> first, for example:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>mkdir fuite-test &amp;&amp; cd fuite-test
npm init -y 
npm i -D fuite
</code></pre>
<p>Now create a <code>fuite.mjs</code> file:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { defaultScenario, findLeaks } from 'fuite'
import fs from 'fs'

const domain = 'http://localhost:8080'
const myScenario = {
  ...defaultScenario,
  async setup(page) {
    let url = await page.url()

    if (!url.endsWith('login')) return

    // Run login code only if we're at /login
    await page.type('input[type=email]', 'email@example.com')
    await page.type('input[type=password]', 'password')
    await page.click('button[type=submit]')
  },
}

let log = []

for await (const result of findLeaks(domain, {
  scenario: myScenario,
  iterations: 5,
  heapsnapshot: false,
  debug: process.env.DEBUG || false,
  progress: true,
  browserArgs: ['--use-fake-device-for-media-stream']
})) {
  const entry = JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)
  // If you want, you can also print the logs while the scanner runs:
  // console.log(entry)
  log.push(entry)
}

console.info('Fuite scan finished:', log.length)

const filename = `fuite.${Date.now()}.json`
console.info('Dumping log file:', filename)
fs.writeFileSync(`logs/${filename}`, JSON.stringify(log, null, 2))
</code></pre>
<p>And you can run it with <code>node fuite.mjs</code>.</p>
<p><strong>NOTE</strong>: If you have a large application with lots of links, you'd be better off to stream to the log/JSON file with <code>writeStream</code>, I'm a little lazy in my example here, so this could be slow for you.</p>

